@page "/vehicle-feeds"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@implements IDisposable
@inject VehicleFeedComparison Comparison
@inject ChargeControlStatusHolder StatusHolder
@inject TimeProvider Time
@inject IEnumerable<IVehicleUpdateService> Feeds

@*
    The week of running both feeds, as numbers (issue #141).

    Phase 3 of #137 is a measurement rather than a feature: the Home Assistant topic and the
    manufacturer's own portal run side by side, and the handover between them is decided on what they
    actually delivered instead of on which one sounded better. This page is where those figures are
    read off -- cadence, agreement, coverage and survival, the four #141 asks for -- and it is the
    thing to copy into docs/DECISIONS.md when the week is up.

    Observation only. Nothing here is read by a charging decision, and a feed comparison that said
    nothing at all would change no register.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="Vehicle feeds" />

<h1>Vehicle feeds</h1>

<p class="muted">
    What each feed has actually delivered, counted rather than eyeballed. Two feeds may run at once —
    the car's own portal and whatever publishes to the MQTT topic — and the holder keeps whichever saw
    the car most recently, so this is also the only place a reading that <em>lost</em> is still
    visible.
</p>

@*
    The window first, because every number below is worth exactly as much as the time it was measured
    over -- and because a restart puts it back to zero, which is a fact about the measurement that has
    to be visible rather than discovered.
*@
<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Measuring for</dt>
    <dd>
        @Duration(_report.ObservedFor)
        <span class="muted">
            (since @TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(_report.ObservingSince, Time.LocalTimeZone).ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm"))
        </span>
    </dd>

    <dt>Feeds seen</dt>
    <dd>
        @if (_report.Sources.Count == 0)
        {
            <span class="muted">none has offered a reading yet</span>
        }
        else
        {
            @string.Join(", ", _report.Sources.Select(source => source.SourceId))
        }
    </dd>
</dl>

<p class="muted table-note">
    Everything on this page is since the controller started. That is deliberate: this measures an
    <em>unattended run</em>, and a gap that spans a restart is the restart rather than the feed —
    folding the two together would turn a redeployment into evidence against a portal. A week means a
    week of one process.
</p>

@if (_report.Sources.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="muted">
        Nothing has been offered yet. The MQTT feed is on when <code>Vehicle__Enabled</code> says so;
        the manufacturer's own feed is on when <code>Vehicle__DataAct__Enabled</code> does. With
        neither, the car is still shown from its configuration and charging works in kilowatt-hours —
        which is a supported installation, not a broken one.
    </p>
}
else
{
    @*
        Cadence. Deliveries, not readings: the portal is asked every fifteen minutes and will hand back
        the bundle it handed back last time, and a retained MQTT message is replayed on every
        reconnect. Counting those as deliveries would report a cadence the car never had.
    *@
    <h2>Cadence</h2>

    <table class="sessions energy">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Feed</th>
                <th class="num">Deliveries</th>
                <th class="num">Repeats</th>
                <th class="num">Went back</th>
                <th class="num">Superseded</th>
                <th class="num">Shortest</th>
                <th class="num">Mean</th>
                <th class="num">Longest</th>
                <th>Last capture</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var source in _report.Sources)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@source.SourceId</td>
                    <td class="num">@source.Captures</td>
                    <td class="num">@source.Repeats</td>
                    <td class="num @(source.HasRegressed ? "error" : null)">
                        @source.Regressions
                        @if (source.WorstRegression is { } worst)
                        {
                            <span class="muted"> (−@Duration(worst))</span>
                        }
                    </td>
                    <td class="num">@source.Superseded</td>
                    <td class="num">@Gap(source.ShortestGap)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Gap(source.MeanGap)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Gap(source.LongestGap)</td>
                    <td>
                        @if (source.LastCapturedAt is { } last)
                        {
                            @TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(last, Time.LocalTimeZone).ToString("MM-dd HH:mm")
                            <span class="muted"> (@Age(last) ago)</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span class="muted">—</span>
                        }
                    </td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="muted table-note">
        A <em>repeat</em> is a reading whose capture time this feed had already produced — the same
        bundle again, or a retained message replayed. Ordinary: a feed with nothing new to say.
        <em>Went back</em> is not ordinary, and is counted apart from it for that reason: the feed
        produced a capture time <strong>older</strong> than one it had already shown us, with the worst
        step backwards beside the count. A portal whose continuous data request has stopped filling
        looks exactly like this and like nothing else — from every other angle it is a quiet car.
        <em>Superseded</em> counts the readings the holder declined because the other feed was already
        ahead: a feed that is nearly always superseded is one the dashboard is not showing, however
        healthy its own page looks.
    </p>

    @*
        And the distribution behind that mean, because one bad night and a bad week look identical in
        a mean and a maximum. The bands are placed around the claim being tested: the portal says a
        quarter of an hour, so the second column is "it kept its word" and the third is "it missed
        one".
    *@
    <h3>Gaps between deliveries</h3>

    <table class="sessions energy">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Feed</th>
                @foreach (var label in BucketLabels)
                {
                    <th class="num">@label</th>
                }
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var source in _report.Sources)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@source.SourceId</td>
                    @foreach (var count in source.GapBuckets)
                    {
                        <td class="num">@count</td>
                    }
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    @*
        Coverage. "Absent is fine, present-but-unusable is not" (#73) means a missing field is a
        supported answer rather than a failure -- so these are coverage figures, and a column of zeroes
        is a finding and not a fault.
    *@
    <h2>Coverage</h2>

    <table class="sessions energy">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Feed</th>
                <th class="num">SOC</th>
                <th class="num">Range</th>
                <th class="num">Time left</th>
                <th class="num">Charge state</th>
                <th class="num">Plug state</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var source in _report.Sources)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@source.SourceId</td>
                    <td class="num">@Coverage(source, source.SocReadings)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Coverage(source, source.RangeReadings)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Coverage(source, source.ChargeTimeReadings)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Coverage(source, source.ChargeStateReadings)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Coverage(source, source.PlugStateReadings)</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="muted table-note">
        The share of deliveries that carried each field. A missing field is a supported answer, not a
        fault — but <em>time left</em> is worth reading closely: nothing has ever established whether
        this car reports a charge-time-remaining at all, and a week of zero is that answer.
    </p>
}

@*
    Agreement. The question is not "do the two numbers differ" -- they will -- but whether one of them
    differs the same way every time, which is what a systematic offset looks like and what would mean
    one of the two is being read wrong.
*@
@if (_report.Pairs.Count > 0)
{
    <h2>Agreement</h2>

    @foreach (var pair in _report.Pairs)
    {
        <h3>@pair.First against @pair.Second</h3>

        <table class="sessions energy">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Comparisons</th>
                    <th class="num">Samples</th>
                    <th class="num">Mean difference</th>
                    <th class="num">Mean size</th>
                    <th class="num">Worst</th>
                    <th class="num">Captures apart</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Car parked</td>
                    <td class="num">@pair.Quiet.Samples</td>
                    <td class="num">@Delta(pair.Quiet)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Points(pair.Quiet.Samples, pair.Quiet.MeanAbsDelta)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Points(pair.Quiet.Samples, pair.Quiet.MaxAbsDelta)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Separation(pair.Quiet)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>All</td>
                    <td class="num">@pair.All.Samples</td>
                    <td class="num">@Delta(pair.All)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Points(pair.All.Samples, pair.All.MeanAbsDelta)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Points(pair.All.Samples, pair.All.MaxAbsDelta)</td>
                    <td class="num">@Separation(pair.All)</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="muted table-note">
            Positive means <strong>@pair.First</strong> reads higher than <strong>@pair.Second</strong>.
            Read the parked row: a charging car really does change between two capture times, so a
            difference measured across that is the car moving on rather than the feeds disagreeing. A
            mean near zero with a non-trivial mean size is noise; a mean that stays a few points off is
            the systematic offset, and it matters which of the two is wrong.
        </p>
    }
}
else if (_report.Sources.Count > 1)
{
    <h2>Agreement</h2>

    <p class="muted">
        Both feeds have delivered, but no two of their capture times have yet landed within
        @Duration(VehicleFeedComparison.PairingWindow) of each other with a state of charge on both —
        so there is nothing to compare. That is itself worth knowing: two feeds this far apart in time
        describe the same battery at different moments, not the same reading twice.
    </p>
}

@*
    Survival, which is the feed's own business and stays on the feed: how many times it has had to
    sign in again. One, however long the controller has been up, is the healthy answer -- the
    measurement #138 could not make because nothing kept a session.
*@
@if (_diagnostics is { Attempts: > 0 } diagnostics && _service is not null)
{
    <h2>Survival</h2>

    <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Feed</dt>
        <dd>@_service.Manufacturer <span class="muted">for @_service.VehicleId</span></dd>

        <dt>Reads</dt>
        <dd>
            @diagnostics.Readings of @diagnostics.Attempts
            @if (diagnostics.LastReadingAt is { } lastRead)
            {
                <span class="muted">— last @Age(lastRead) ago</span>
            }
        </dd>

        <dt>Sign-ins</dt>
        <dd class="@(diagnostics.Sessions > 1 ? "error" : null)">
            @diagnostics.Sessions
            <span class="muted">
                @(diagnostics.Sessions <= 1
                    ? "— one session has covered the whole run"
                    : "— sessions are expiring and the feed is re-logging in")
            </span>
        </dd>

        <dt>Session age</dt>
        <dd>@(diagnostics.SessionAge is { } age ? Duration(age) : "none")</dd>

        @*
            The one field #137 promised that VehicleState deliberately does not carry. Whether it
            arrives at all is a Phase 3 question, and this feed is the only thing that ever sees it.
        *@
        <dt>Target SOC</dt>
        <dd>
            @if (diagnostics.TargetSocReadings == 0)
            {
                <span class="muted">never reported — the portal carries the field, this car has not</span>
            }
            else
            {
                @(diagnostics.TargetSocPercent is { } target ? $"{target:0.#}%" : "—")
                <span class="muted">
                    (on @diagnostics.TargetSocReadings of @diagnostics.Readings read@(diagnostics.Readings == 1 ? "" : "s"))
                </span>
            }
        </dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="muted table-note">
        Nothing reads the target SOC yet. It is the figure that would let a plan refuse an impossible
        battery target, deferred on
        <a href="https://github.com/mpospisil/gleanvolt/issues/101">#101</a> because no feed carried it
        reliably — which is the question this row exists to answer.
    </p>
}

<h2>When the week is boring</h2>

<p class="muted">
    The handover is a setting, not a deletion. Switch <code>Vehicle__Enabled</code> to
    <code>false</code>, stop the Home Assistant vehicle automation, and leave
    <code>Vehicle__DataAct__Enabled</code> on. No code goes away: if the portal disappoints in month
    two, the old feed is one setting away, and it stays the answer for any car nobody has written a
    service for. If the week is <em>not</em> boring, that is a result too — record it and stay on
    MQTT.
</p>

@code {
    private VehicleFeedReport _report = VehicleFeedReport.Empty;

    // The manufacturer feed, when one is configured: the survival figures are its own and are asked
    // for with a type test, because a feed whose API pushes has neither a session nor a next-due time.
    private IVehicleUpdateService? _service;
    private VehicleFeedDiagnostics? _diagnostics;

    private static readonly string[] BucketLabels = BuildBucketLabels();

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _service = Feeds.FirstOrDefault();
        Refresh();

        // Refreshed on the control loop's cadence rather than on a timer of its own. The figures here
        // move on the feeds' clocks -- a quarter of an hour at best -- so anything faster would be
        // re-rendering a page that has not changed.
        StatusHolder.Updated += OnStatusUpdated;
    }

    private void OnStatusUpdated(ChargeControlStatus status)
    {
        Refresh();
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private void Refresh()
    {
        _report = Comparison.Report();
        _diagnostics = (_service as IVehicleFeedDiagnostics)?.Diagnostics;
    }

    public void Dispose() => StatusHolder.Updated -= OnStatusUpdated;

    private static string[] BuildBucketLabels()
    {
        var edges = VehicleFeedComparison.GapBucketEdges;
        var labels = new string[edges.Count + 1];

        for (var index = 0; index < edges.Count; index++)
        {
            labels[index] = $"≤ {Short(edges[index])}";
        }

        labels[edges.Count] = $"> {Short(edges[^1])}";
        return labels;
    }

    // Bucket edges are round figures by construction, so this is allowed to be terse in a way the
    // measured durations below are not.
    private static string Short(TimeSpan span) =>
        span < TimeSpan.FromHours(1) ? $"{span.TotalMinutes:0} min" : $"{span.TotalHours:0} h";

    private static string Gap(TimeSpan? gap) => gap is { } span ? Duration(span) : "—";

    /// <summary>
    /// A duration at the accuracy the thing being measured actually has. Minutes for anything under
    /// an hour, because a feed's cadence is a matter of minutes; days and hours above that, because
    /// "measuring for 4 d 6 h" is the sentence, and "4 d 6 h 13 m" is not.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Duration(TimeSpan span)
    {
        if (span < TimeSpan.Zero)
        {
            span = TimeSpan.Zero;
        }

        if (span < TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1))
        {
            return $"{span.TotalSeconds:0} s";
        }

        if (span < TimeSpan.FromHours(1))
        {
            return $"{span.TotalMinutes:0} min";
        }

        if (span < TimeSpan.FromDays(1))
        {
            return $"{span.Hours} h {span.Minutes:00} m";
        }

        return $"{(int)span.TotalDays} d {span.Hours} h";
    }

    private string Age(DateTimeOffset at) => Duration(Time.GetUtcNow() - at);

    private static string Coverage(VehicleFeedTally source, int readings) =>
        source.Coverage(readings) is { } share ? $"{share:P0}" : "—";

    // Signed on purpose: the direction is the finding, and dropping it would average a systematic
    // offset away to nothing.
    private static string Delta(VehicleSocAgreement agreement) =>
        agreement.Samples == 0 ? "—" : $"{agreement.MeanDelta:+0.0;-0.0;0.0} pt";

    private static string Points(int samples, double value) =>
        samples == 0 ? "—" : $"{value:0.0} pt";

    private static string Separation(VehicleSocAgreement agreement) =>
        agreement.Samples == 0 ? "—" : Duration(agreement.MeanSeparation);
}
